Here the traversal records every node as `(column, depth, order, value)`. After the traversal, the records are sorted by column, then depth, then visit order. For each column, the final record is the one that should appear in the bottom view.

The extra sorting makes the code a little longer than a direct map solution, but it makes the selection rule visible in the data. An empty tree returns an empty slice.

```go
import "sort"

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * type TreeNode struct {
 *     Data int
 *     Left *TreeNode
 *     Right *TreeNode
 * }
 */

type viewNode struct {
    node   *TreeNode
    column int
    depth  int
    order  int
}

type viewRecord struct {
    column int
    depth  int
    order  int
    value  int
}

func bottomView(root *TreeNode) []int {
    if root == nil {
        return []int{}
    }

    queue := []viewNode{{node: root, column: 0, depth: 0}}
    records := make([]viewRecord, 0)
    order := 0

    for len(queue) > 0 {
        current := queue[0]
        queue = queue[1:]

        records = append(records, viewRecord{
            column: current.column,
            depth:  current.depth,
            order:  order,
            value:  current.node.Data,
        })
        order++

        if current.node.Left != nil {
            queue = append(queue, viewNode{
                node: current.node.Left,
                column: current.column - 1,
                depth: current.depth + 1,
            })
        }
        if current.node.Right != nil {
            queue = append(queue, viewNode{
                node: current.node.Right,
                column: current.column + 1,
                depth: current.depth + 1,
            })
        }
    }

    sort.Slice(records, func(i, j int) bool {
        if records[i].column != records[j].column {
            return records[i].column < records[j].column
        }
        if records[i].depth != records[j].depth {
            return records[i].depth < records[j].depth
        }
        return records[i].order < records[j].order
    })

    answer := make([]int, 0)
    for i := 0; i < len(records); {
        j := i
        for j+1 < len(records) && records[j+1].column == records[i].column {
            j++
        }
        answer = append(answer, records[j].value)
        i = j + 1
    }

    return answer
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` for sorting the recorded nodes.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the queue and record slice.

A queue keeps the tree in top-to-bottom order. I attach a horizontal distance to every node, where the root is at 0, the left child decreases it, and the right child increases it. When a node is visited, its value replaces the old value in that column. Because BFS visits deeper levels later, the last value stored for a column is the visible bottom value.

For example, if two nodes have horizontal distances -1 and 1, their values go into those two map keys. The `map` also gives the columns from left to right when we build the answer.

```cpp
#include <map>
#include <queue>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
  public:
    vector <int> bottomView(TreeNode *root){
        vector<int> answer;
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return answer;
        }

        map<int, int> columnValue;
        queue<pair<TreeNode*, int>> nodes;
        nodes.push({root, 0});

        while (!nodes.empty()) {
            auto current = nodes.front();
            nodes.pop();

            TreeNode* node = current.first;
            int column = current.second;
            columnValue[column] = node->data;

            if (node->left != nullptr) {
                nodes.push({node->left, column - 1});
            }
            if (node->right != nullptr) {
                nodes.push({node->right, column + 1});
            }
        }

        for (const auto& entry : columnValue) {
            answer.push_back(entry.second);
        }
        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because the map stores up to `n` columns.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the queue and map.

This version uses DFS instead of a queue. Each column stores two things: the deepest level seen there and its value. A node replaces the stored entry when it is deeper, or when it is at the same depth but visited later. The second condition handles a common tie rule for bottom view.

The tree itself is not changed. A `TreeMap` keeps the final columns sorted automatically. For a tree with only one node, the root is simply stored at column 0.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.TreeMap;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    private static class Entry {
        int depth;
        int value;

        Entry(int depth, int value) {
            this.depth = depth;
            this.value = value;
        }
    }

    public List<Integer> bottomView(TreeNode root) {
        Map<Integer, Entry> columns = new TreeMap<>();
        visit(root, 0, 0, columns);

        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Entry entry : columns.values()) {
            answer.add(entry.value);
        }
        return answer;
    }

    private void visit(TreeNode node, int column, int depth,
                       Map<Integer, Entry> columns) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        Entry old = columns.get(column);
        if (old == null || depth >= old.depth) {
            columns.put(column, new Entry(depth, node.data));
        }

        visit(node.left, column - 1, depth + 1, columns);
        visit(node.right, column + 1, depth + 1, columns);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` due to the sorted map.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the map and recursive call stack. A very skewed tree can make the stack `O(n)`.

A recursive walk can also handle the columns. The helper receives the current horizontal distance and depth. For every column, it keeps the value from the greatest depth. If another node has the same depth, it replaces the old one because it is visited later in the left-to-right DFS order.

Dry run: starting at column 0, moving left changes the column to -1 and moving right changes it to 1. Once the recursion finishes, the `map` contains one selected value for every column. The code only reads the input tree.

```cpp
#include <map>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
  public:
    vector <int> bottomView(TreeNode *root){
        map<int, pair<int, int>> columns;
        collect(root, 0, 0, columns);

        vector<int> answer;
        for (const auto& entry : columns) {
            answer.push_back(entry.second.second);
        }
        return answer;
    }

  private:
    void collect(TreeNode* node, int column, int depth,
                 map<int, pair<int, int>>& columns) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return;
        }

        auto found = columns.find(column);
        if (found == columns.end() || depth >= found->second.first) {
            columns[column] = {depth, node->data};
        }

        collect(node->left, column - 1, depth + 1, columns);
        collect(node->right, column + 1, depth + 1, columns);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` from map updates.
Space complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case, including the map and recursive stack.

